---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import SnapshotLab from "../../components/SnapshotLab.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomUrl, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

const lang = "en";
const r = room("snapshots");
const DS = "module/zfs/dsl_dataset.c";
const DL = "module/zfs/dsl_deadlist.c";
const DD = "module/zfs/dsl_destroy.c";
const L = {
  birthMacro: gh("include/sys/spa.h", 505, 507),
  logical: gh("include/sys/spa.h", 483, 492),
  words: gh("include/sys/spa.h", 147, 149),
  alloc: gh("module/zfs/metaslab.c", 6061),
  allocTxg: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 4380, 4382),
  dbufDone: gh("module/zfs/dbuf.c", 5149, 5155),
  objsetDone: gh("module/zfs/dmu_objset.c", 1535, 1543),
  freeBlocks: gh("module/zfs/dnode_sync.c", 147),
  pending: gh(DS, 333, 342),
  syncDone: gh(DS, 2292, 2293),
  prevTxg: gh("include/sys/dsl_dataset.h", 142, 143),
  setPrev: gh(DS, 1881, 1884),
  dlClone: gh(DL, 727, 737),
  notInclusive: gh(DL, 521, 524),
  zapKey: gh(DL, 544, 545),
  eagain: gh(DS, 1534, 1539),
  tasks: gh("module/zfs/dsl_pool.c", 845, 854),
  originNull: gh(DS, 1186, 1187),
  originTxg: gh(DS, 1752, 1758),
  createOrigin: gh("module/zfs/dsl_pool.c", 1179, 1183),
  numChildren: gh(DS, 1243, 1244),
  cloneDl: gh(DS, 1246, 1251),
  originObj: gh(DS, 1264, 1265),
  ddOriginTxg: gh("module/zfs/dsl_dir.c", 269, 270),
  moveBpobj: gh(DL, 930, 952),
  freeBpobj: gh("module/zfs/dsl_scan.c", 3828, 3835),
  insertBpobj: gh(DL, 829, 834),
  dlFree: gh(DD, 434, 437),
  removeKey: gh(DL, 549, 577),
  clonesKey: gh(DD, 441, 445),
  clonesKeyTest: gh(DD, 230, 232),
  headKey: gh(DD, 475, 479),
  branch: gh(DD, 83, 87),
  oldFmt: gh(DD, 129, 151),
  livelist: gh(DS, 285, 292),
  promote: gh(DS, 3713, 3721),
  numChildrenField: gh("include/sys/dsl_dataset.h", 146),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="snapshots"
  title="Snapshots and clones · Uberblok"
  description="How a ZFS snapshot costs one block pointer, and how one comparison of birth txgs decides whether a dead block is freed or kept on a deadlist: interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)}</p>
      <h1>{r.title.en}</h1>
      <div class="prose problem">
        <p class="lead">
          A snapshot has to freeze a whole filesystem at once, a terabyte as easily as a kilobyte, and must not slow the
          writes that follow. Taking it is the easy half. The hard half comes at every later overwrite and every delete in
          the live filesystem: is the old block still needed by a snapshot, or can its space be reused? Asking every
          snapshot would mean scanning them. A reference count on every block would mean updating counters for every
          block at every snapshot.
        </p>
        <p>
          ZFS does neither. Every block pointer records the txg in which the block was <em>born</em>. The live filesystem
          remembers the txg of its latest snapshot. A block born after that snapshot cannot be in any snapshot, so it is
          freed; a block born at or before it may be, so it is kept, on a <em>deadlist</em>. One comparison of two
          numbers.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="lab">
      <h2 id="lab"><span class="n">1</span> Write, snapshot, sync</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <code>fs</code> is a filesystem of four data blocks, A to D, under one root block, the objset its
          <code>ds_bp</code> points to. Each block shows its birth txg: A<sub>8</sub> was born in txg 8. The figure opens
          after three txgs: A, B and C written in txg 8, the snapshot <code>fs@s1</code> taken in txg 9, A overwritten in
          txg 10. Queue changes in the open txg, take a snapshot, clone one or destroy one, then sync: every block the
          sync lets go of shows the comparison and where it goes. Select any block to see which snapshots hold it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <SnapshotLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="depths">
      <h2 id="depths">The idea, at three depths</h2>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one sentence</h3>
        <p class="lead prose">
          Every block carries the txg of its birth, so when the live filesystem lets go of a block, one comparison with
          the txg of its latest snapshot decides: free it, or keep it on a deadlist for the snapshots that still hold it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one paragraph</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A snapshot is a new dataset record that copies one block pointer, <code>ds_bp</code>, the root of the
          filesystem’s tree, and nothing else: every block below is now shared. <a href={roomUrl(lang, "copy-on-write")}>Copy-on-write</a> never modifies those
          blocks again; it writes new ones, born in later txgs, and <em>kills</em> the old ones.
          <code>dsl_dataset_block_kill</code> compares the old block’s birth with the filesystem’s
          <code>ds_prev_snap_txg</code>, the txg of its latest snapshot. Born later, the block belongs to the live
          filesystem alone and is freed. Born at or before, the latest snapshot holds it, and it goes on the deadlist. The
          deadlist is cut into entries by the txgs of earlier snapshots, so it says between which two snapshots each dead
          block was born. That makes destroying a snapshot cheap: the blocks only it held are whole entries of the next
          dataset’s deadlist, freed without reading one block pointer. A clone is a writable dataset that starts from a
          snapshot’s <code>ds_bp</code>, with that snapshot’s txg as its own <code>ds_prev_snap_txg</code>: the same
          comparison keeps the blocks it shares with its origin and frees its own.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In detail</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            <strong>Birth.</strong> A block’s birth txg is set when it is allocated: <code>metaslab_alloc</code> ends with
            <a href={L.alloc}><code>BP_SET_BIRTH(bp, txg, 0)</code></a>, and the write passes it
            <a href={L.allocTxg}>the txg it belongs to</a>. A block pointer has <a href={L.words}>two birth words</a>,
            logical and physical; they differ only for <a href={L.logical}>dedup, block cloning and rewritten blocks</a>,
            and the value compared below is <a href={L.birthMacro}><code>BP_GET_BIRTH</code></a>, the logical birth for
            an ordinary block.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Free now, or deadlist.</strong> Here is the whole decision.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={DS}
          from={294}
          to={316}
          symbol="dsl_dataset_block_kill"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 294,
              text: "The comparison. <code>ds_prev_snap_txg</code> is the txg of the dataset’s latest snapshot. The test is strict: a block born in that very txg is held by that snapshot.",
            },
            {
              from: 304,
              to: 311,
              text: "Gang, dedup and possibly cloned blocks are queued on the pool’s free list instead of being freed inline, because freeing them costs I/O. The outcome is the same: no snapshot holds them.",
            },
            {
              from: 312,
              to: 315,
              text: "The ordinary case: <code>dsl_free</code>, in this txg. The figure greys the block out under “Freed”.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Otherwise, in the <code>else</code> branch, the block <a href={L.pending}>goes onto the deadlist</a>. Three
            callers kill blocks, all while a txg syncs: <a href={L.dbufDone}><code>dbuf_write_done</code></a> when a data or
            indirect block was rewritten, <a href={L.objsetDone}><code>dmu_objset_write_done</code></a> for the objset block
            that <code>ds_bp</code> points to, rewritten in every txg in which the dataset changes, and
            <a href={L.freeBlocks}><code>free_blocks</code></a> for a delete or a truncation. The figure draws that objset
            and the indirect blocks as one root, killed by the same rule. The two write callbacks park a deadlist-bound
            block on a pending list that <a href={L.syncDone}><code>dsl_dataset_sync_done</code></a> empties before the
            txg’s sync tasks: same outcome, a few lines later.
          </p>
          <p><strong>A snapshot copies one block pointer.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={DS}
          from={1760}
          to={1785}
          symbol="dsl_dataset_snapshot_sync_impl"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 1760,
              to: 1761,
              text: "One new object in the MOS, the pool’s own metadata: the snapshot’s dataset record.",
            },
            {
              from: 1770,
              to: 1773,
              text: "It takes the head’s link to the previous snapshot and its <code>ds_prev_snap_txg</code>, points forward to the head, and starts with one child, its successor.",
            },
            {
              from: 1775,
              text: "Its creation txg is the txg being synced.",
            },
            {
              from: 1776,
              text: "The head’s deadlist becomes the snapshot’s, as it is.",
            },
            {
              from: 1783,
              text: "The root block pointer is copied. That one copy is what makes the snapshot share every block with the head. Nothing in the tree is read or written: the figure shows no new block for a snapshot.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            <strong>The head’s new deadlist.</strong> Further down the same function, the head gets a deadlist of its
            own.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={DS}
          from={1830}
          to={1839}
          symbol="dsl_dataset_snapshot_sync_impl"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 1831,
              to: 1833,
              text: `<code>dsl_deadlist_clone</code> with no limit: <a href="${L.dlClone}">one empty entry for every key</a> of the deadlist the snapshot just took.`,
            },
            {
              from: 1837,
              to: 1838,
              text: `Plus one key: the head’s <code>ds_prev_snap_txg</code>, which here is still the txg of the <em>previous</em> snapshot. It is set to this snapshot’s txg <a href="${L.setPrev}">only afterwards</a>.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            So after snapshots in txgs t1 &lt; t2 &lt; … &lt; tn, the head’s deadlist has its starting keys plus t1 to
            t(n−1), and its <a href={L.prevTxg}><code>ds_prev_snap_txg</code></a> is tn. Each snapshot keeps the deadlist the
            head had until it was taken: the blocks killed between the previous snapshot and this one. That is, blocks the
            <em>previous</em> snapshot holds and this one does not; a snapshot’s deadlist is not “the blocks only it owns”.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Where a dead block goes.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={DL}
          from={487}
          to={501}
          symbol="dsl_deadlist_insert"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 487,
              to: 492,
              text: `A deadlist is a tree of entries, each a list of block pointers <a href="${L.zapKey}">stored under a key</a>, a txg that <a href="${L.notInclusive}">“is not inclusive”</a>. Look the birth up: on an exact match take the entry before it, otherwise the nearest entry before. Either way, the entry with the largest key strictly below the birth.`,
            },
            {
              from: 494,
              to: 498,
              text: "A birth at or below every key cannot happen on a sound pool: it is reported, and the block goes to the first entry.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            <strong>Where the keys start.</strong> A filesystem is made <a href={L.originNull}>from the pool’s
            <code>$ORIGIN</code> snapshot</a>, created <a href={L.createOrigin}>from an empty dataset</a> with
            <a href={L.originTxg}>creation txg 1</a>. Traced through the excerpts, a new filesystem starts with
            <code>ds_prev_snap_txg</code> 1 and a deadlist with one key, 0, and its first snapshot adds key 1. Every
            block a user writes is born after txg 1: entry 0 stays empty, entry 1 collects what was born before the first
            snapshot. These are the <code>&gt; 0</code> and <code>&gt; 1</code> entries of the figure.
          </p>
          <p><strong>The same txg.</strong> A snapshot is a sync task, and the txg writes its data first.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/dsl_pool.c"
          from={747}
          to={760}
          symbol="dsl_pool_sync"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 747,
              to: 753,
              text: `Every dirty dataset is written out before the sync tasks run, “so that … we won’t get confused about which blocks are part of the snapshot”. The tasks run <a href="${L.tasks}">at the end</a>.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Two consequences, both in the figure. A block written in the same txg as a snapshot is in that snapshot: born in
            txg T, held by the snapshot of txg T, which is why the test is strict. And a block killed in the txg of a
            snapshot is not in it: the kill was decided against the previous snapshot. One snapshot of a dataset per txg:
            a second one <a href={L.eagain}>is refused, <code>EAGAIN</code></a>.
          </p>
          <p><strong>A clone starts from a snapshot’s block pointer.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={DS}
          from={1213}
          to={1227}
          symbol="dsl_dataset_create_sync_dd"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 1216,
              to: 1218,
              text: "The clone’s previous snapshot is its origin, and its <code>ds_prev_snap_txg</code> the origin’s creation txg.",
            },
            {
              from: 1225,
              to: 1227,
              text: "Its <code>ds_bp</code> is the origin’s: like a snapshot, a clone starts as one copied block pointer.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Then the origin <a href={L.numChildren}>gains a child</a>, the clone’s deadlist
            <a href={L.cloneDl}>starts with the keys below the origin’s txg</a>, and the origin is recorded
            <a href={L.originObj}>in the clone’s directory</a> (in memory, <a href={L.ddOriginTxg}><code>dd_origin_txg</code></a>).
            A write in the clone goes through the same <code>dsl_dataset_block_kill</code>, against the clone’s own
            <code>ds_prev_snap_txg</code>: a block the clone wrote is born after the origin and freed when the clone lets
            go of it; a block born at or before the origin is shared with it and goes on the clone’s deadlist. The clone
            diverges by birth txg, and nothing is copied.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Destroying a snapshot.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={DD}
          from={387}
          to={424}
          symbol="dsl_destroy_snapshot_sync_impl"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 387,
              to: 391,
              text: "The next dataset, the next snapshot or the head, inherits the destroyed snapshot’s previous snapshot and <code>ds_prev_snap_txg</code>.",
            },
            {
              from: 395,
              to: 397,
              text: `Pools that predate keyed deadlists <a href="${L.oldFmt}">apply the same test to each block</a>. The figure follows the keyed path.`,
            },
            {
              from: 399,
              to: 413,
              text: "Space accounting, not drawn.",
            },
            {
              from: 415,
              to: 418,
              text: `Every entry of the next deadlist <a href="${L.moveBpobj}">at or above</a> the snapshot’s <code>ds_prev_snap_txg</code> goes to the pool’s free list. Those blocks were born after the snapshot before it and killed before the next one: only this snapshot held them. The keys alone say so. The sync thread <a href="${L.freeBpobj}">frees that list</a> in later txgs; the figure frees them at once.`,
            },
            {
              from: 422,
              to: 424,
              text: `The snapshot’s own deadlist, blocks the snapshot before it still holds, joins the next one, <a href="${L.insertBpobj}">entry by entry</a>, and is then <a href="${L.dlFree}">freed</a>.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Last, the key of the snapshot’s txg <a href={L.removeKey}>is removed, its entry merged into the one before</a>:
            from the head <a href={L.headKey}>when the next dataset is a snapshot</a>, and, through
            <a href={L.clonesKey}><code>dsl_dir_remove_clones_key</code></a>, from every clone
            <a href={L.clonesKeyTest}>whose origin is younger than the snapshot</a>. A snapshot with a
            clone has <a href={L.numChildrenField}>more than one child</a> and <a href={L.branch}>cannot be destroyed,
            <code>EEXIST</code></a>: “Can’t delete a branch point”.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Which snapshot owns this block.</strong> Nothing in ZFS stores that answer: it follows from birth txgs.
            A block of <code>fs</code> born in txg B and killed in txg K is held by the snapshots of <code>fs</code> taken from
            txg B to txg K − 1: those taken before B were frozen before it existed, those taken in K or later after it was
            gone. A block still live is held by every snapshot taken since B. The figure computes that range from the
            numbers, and its tests check it against which dataset’s tree actually holds the block, over thousands of
            random sequences.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figure runs the functions above over one filesystem with four data blocks and up to four snapshots, and one
        clone that cannot itself be snapshotted or destroyed. The objset, dnodes and indirect blocks are one root block.
        Deferred frees of gang, dedup and cloned blocks, space accounting, and the clone’s
        <a href={L.livelist}>livelist</a> are left out; none changes whether a block is freed or kept. Blocks on the pool’s
        free list are drawn as freed at once. Holds, deferred destroy and old-format deadlists are not shown. Bookmarks
        (<code>dsl_bookmark.c</code>) and <code>zfs promote</code>, which <a href={L.promote}>swaps a clone and its
        origin’s filesystem</a>, are other rooms’ business.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="snapshots" />
</Base>

